{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529303458,"sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2481af6002OHL7PLSBbqiBpm","sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","messageID":"msg_1d24811670012plG2d3pwnESdl","type":"tool","id":"call_4af0b97248f44c21867d7b84","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/tasks.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/tasks.md, lines 1-253\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 10: reachability-and-attribution\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that record the offer and the brief on every run line, backfill the before arm, make the scorer attributable, add the Codex and DSH install steps with read-only doctor checks and measure the cold first prompt before changing it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reachability and attribution tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 10: reachability-and-attribution\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `S` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs`. `R` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs`. `T` is `$ADV/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts`.\n32: - `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs` (new). `OT` is `$ADV/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts` (new). `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n33: - `VT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `VO` is `npx vitest run tests/command-offer.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `PRE` is `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/scratch/runs-pre.jsonl`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n35: - `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`, the Claude prompt hook entry.\n36: - `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `DI` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs`. `DT` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/tests/bundle.test.mjs`. `DR` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md`. `DA` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/README.md`.\n37: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `CK` is `$DS/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` and `CKT` its test `$DS/tests/codex-hook-approval-check.test.cjs`. `HW` is `$DS/hook-wiring-check.cjs` and `HWT` its test `$DS/tests/hook-wiring-check.test.cjs`.\n38: - `RMY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-runtime-mirrors.yaml`. `SCY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-symlink-check.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs`.\n39: - `HR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/hook-registry.json`. `HS` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs`. `LCH` is `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-launcher.cjs`.\n40: - `FLOOR` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n41: \n42: Rules for every task:\n43: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a script and its test adds the behaviour and the one case that pins it.\n44: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A row whose split is `holdout` is never opened.\n45: - No worker starts a live model session. Live runs, arenas and operator steps are `[C]` tasks.\n46: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T028 checks it.\n47: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md` or renames `.skilled/commands/create/skill.md`.\n48: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n49: \n50: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n51: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`, `$SP/summary-before.json`), T002 (`$SP/socket-key.txt`), T003 (`$SP/cold-start.mjs`, `$SP/cold-start-before.txt`). Read-only for the repository.\n52: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that the before arm in `PRE` is finished, because T017 changes what the start rate counts.\n53: - Wave 1: T004 (`OF`, `OT`), T005 (`S`, `T`), T006 (`DI`, `DT`), T007 (`CK`, `CKT`), T008 (`HW`, `HWT`), T009 (`IG`).\n54: - Wave 2: T010 (`OF`, `OT`), T011 (`S`, `T`), T012 (`IG`), T013 (`DR`, `DA`), T014 (`RMY`), T015 (`SCY`).\n55: - Wave 3: T016 (`OF`, `OT`), T017 (`S`, `T`), T018 (`$DS/README.markdown`).\n56: - Wave 4: T019 (`R`), T020 (`S`, `T`).\n57: - Wave 5: T021 (`R`), T022 (`S`, `T`).\n58: - Wave 6: T023 (`RA`).\n59: - Cold-start wave C1, only when T003 shows a fail-open: T024 (`HR`, then `HS` regenerates `.claude/settings.json` and `.codex/hooks.json`), T025 (`LCH`).\n60: - Cold-start wave C2: T026 (`$SP/cold-start-after.txt`).\n61: - Verification, one at a time: T027, T028.\n62: - After the phase is committed: T029, T030, T031 and T033 are coordinator tasks, T032 is a worker task that waits on T031 and T034 closes the phase.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [ ] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`, `$SP/summary-before.json`). Run `FLOOR`, `TC`, `VT`, `DCT`, `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards`, `node $HS --check` and `node $S --summary $PRE --json > $SP/summary-before.json`, and write each command with its last output line into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TC` exits 0, `VT` reports 0 failed, `DCT` reports `fail 0`, the bundle test reports `fail 0`, `node $HS --check` exits 0 and `jq -r 'keys | join(\",\")' $SP/summary-before.json` prints the six runtimes.\n71: - [x] T002 Read how `$ADV/skill-advisor-cli.ts` and `LCH` name the daemon socket under `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR`, and record whether two checkouts reach two daemons (`$SP/socket-key.txt`). Verify: the file names the function and the line that derive the socket name and ends with one word, `per-checkout` or `shared`. When it says `shared`, T016 gives each checkout its own socket directory.\n72: - [x] T003 Write `$SP/cold-start.mjs` and run it once with `--runs 5` (`$SP/cold-start-before.txt`). Each run makes a fresh directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cold-'))`, with `tmpdir` from `node:os`, pipes `{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\",\"cwd\":\"<repository root>\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cold-<n>\"}` into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory, and prints the milliseconds and the first line of `additionalContext` (or `<none>`). An optional `--warm` runs `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root <root> --format json --no-warm-only` detached with the same variable and waits 3,000 ms before the hook. After each run the script sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory, then removes the directory. The last line reads `fail-open N of 5`, where a run fails open when its first line does not start with `Advisor: live` or `Advisor: stale`. Verify: the file has six lines and its last line matches `fail-open [0-5] of 5`, `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cold-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing and `ls /tmp/system-skill-advisor | wc -l` prints the same number before and after the run. When N is 0, mark T024 to T026 `[x]` with `Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5` and the median milliseconds.\n73: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n78: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n79: \n80: ### Wave 1\n81: - [x] T004 [P] Create `OF` with two exports. `parseOffer(head)` returns `{ offeredCommand, offeredPolicy }`: `offeredCommand` is the first match of `/\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/` in the head, or null, and `offeredPolicy` is `run`, `start` or `ask` when `(run)`, `(start)` or `(ask)` follows that match after optional spaces, or null. `replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = 30000 })` runs `node <checkoutDir>/$HOOK` with `cwd` `checkoutDir` and the payload shape of T003, takes the first line of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` and returns `parseOffer` of it with `offerError: null`, or null fields with `offerError` `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`. Create `OT` with cases for the heads `Advisor: live; use sk-git → /doctor:git hooks (ask) 0.82/0.12 pass.`, `Advisor: live; ambiguous: /doctor:git hooks 0.82/0.12 vs sk-git 0.86/0.16 pass.`, `Advisor: live; use command-spec-kit-plan 0.82/0.16 pass.` and a `replayOffer` call on a folder with no hook, which returns `offerError` `hook_missing`. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n82: - [x] T005 [P] In `commandParts` (`S`), when the name holds whitespace, keep the text before the first space as `name` and the rest as `route`, so `/doctor:git hooks` looks up `doctor/git.md`. Add one case to `T`: events that read `.skilled/commands/doctor/git.md` and then write give `startState(events, { expected_command: '/doctor:git hooks', expected_policy: 'run' }).started` true. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [x] T006 [P] In `apply` (`DI`), change the advisor flag to `enabled: options.promptAdvisor !== false`. In `DT`, rename the test to `prompt advisor stays on unless its row flag is false` and assert that `preStepCount({ adapters: { promptAdvisor: true } })` equals `preStepCount()` and that `preStepCount({ adapters: { promptAdvisor: false } })` is one lower. Verify: `rg -n \"promptAdvisor !== false\" $DI` prints one line, and `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards` reports `fail 0`.\n84: - [x] T007 [P] Create `CK` and `CKT`. `CK [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]` reads `<root>/.codex/hooks.json` and prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks` with exit 0 when it is absent. It finds the `UserPromptSubmit` group `g` and hook index `i` whose `command` contains `hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`, or prints `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` with exit 1. It reads `--config`, else `${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/config.toml`, and looks for a header line `[hooks.state.\"<root>/.codex/hooks.json:user_prompt_submit:<g>:<i>\"]`, with `<root>` as given or as its real path, followed by a `trusted_hash` line before the next header. Found prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` with exit 0. Otherwise it prints `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` and `repair: open /hooks in a Codex session at <root> and approve each entry`, with exit 1. A bad flag exits 2. It never prints a hash and calls no write, mkdir, rename or remove function. `CKT` covers an approved fixture, an unapproved fixture and a root with no `.codex`. Verify: `node --test $CKT` reports `pass 3` and `fail 0`, `rg -n \"writeFile|appendFile|mkdir|rename|rmSync|unlink\" $CK` prints nothing, and `shasum -a 256 ~/.codex/config.toml` prints the same digest before and after `node $CK --root .`, which prints one line starting `PASS` or `WARN`.\n85: - [x] T008 [P] Create `HW` and `HWT`. `HW --root <dir> [--json]` checks four runtimes: `claude` (`.claude/settings.json`, event `UserPromptSubmit`, marker `hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`), `codex` (`.codex/hooks.json`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`), `cursor` (`.cursor/hooks.json`, `beforeSubmitPrompt`, `hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js`) and `devin` (`.devin/hooks.v1.json`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js`). A runtime whose folder is absent is skipped. It reads the event under `hooks` or at the top level and prints `ok <runtime>` when its JSON text holds the marker, else `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, also for a missing or unreadable file. It exits 1 when any line is `hook-missing`, 0 otherwise and 2 on a bad flag or a missing root. It writes nothing. `HWT` covers a root whose `.claude/settings.json` lacks the entry, the same root with it and a root with no runtime folder. Verify: `node --test $HWT` reports `pass 3` and `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n86: - [x] T009 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the line about hyphenated commands: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved. Verify: `rg -n \"codex-hooks-approved\" $IG` prints one line and `rg -c \"trusted_hash\" $IG` prints 1.\n87: \n88: ### Wave 2\n89: - [x] T010 [P] Add `briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession })` to `OF`. For `claude` it reads `locator.transcriptPath` and returns true when a line has `type` `attachment`, `attachment.type` `hook_additional_context`, `attachment.hookEvent` `UserPromptSubmit` and content matching `/Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/`, false when the file loads without one and `\"unknown\"` when it cannot be read. For `pi` it loads the session and tests its records with the same pattern. Every other runtime returns `\"unknown\"`. Add three `OT` cases: a Claude fixture with the attachment, one without it and a `devin` locator. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed.\n90: - [x] T011 [P] In `S`, carry `wrote` (true when the run line's `changedPaths` is a non-empty array) on each scored result and add `byPolicy` to each tally block with keys `run`, `start` and `ask`, each `{ rows, capped, started, wrote }`, filled from the row's `expected_policy`. Print one text line per policy under each arm, `policy <name>: <rows> rows, <capped> capped, <started> started, <wrote> wrote`. Add one `T` case through the summary fixture. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.claude.pre.byPolicy | keys'` prints `[\"ask\",\"run\",\"start\"]`.\n91: - [x] T012 [P] In `IG`, change `any of the six runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin or Pi` to `any of the seven runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi or DeepSeek Harness (DSH)`, add DSH to the runtimes that spell commands with a hyphen and add one sentence: DSH loads the repository's guards and the skill advisor only from the patches a run names, so start it with `--patch .dsh/patches/guards.patch.yml`. Verify: `grep -ci dsh $IG` prints at least 2.\n92: - [x] T013 [P] In `DR` section 5 and in the `prompt-advisor.mjs` row of `DA`, state that the bundle registers the prompt advisor unless its row flag is `false`, that the guards patch still writes `true` and that a lineage leaf stays without a brief. Verify: `rg -n \"unless its row flag is .false.\" $DR $DA` prints two lines and `rg -n \"Off unless\" $DA` prints nothing.\n93: - [x] T014 [P] In `RMY`, add `codex_hook_approval: \".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs\"` to `upstream_assets` and one step after the `codex_hooks_installer` step: run `upstream_assets.codex_hook_approval` with `--root \"$PWD\"`. `PASS` is in sync, `WARN` sets STATUS to WARN and shows the repair line without counting as drift, and `SKIP` leaves STATUS unchanged. Keep every existing phrase. Verify: `rg -c \"codex_hook_approval\" $RMY` prints 2 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n94: - [x] T015 [P] In `SCY`, add `hook_wiring: \".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs\"` to `upstream_assets`, add a step after the engine run that runs `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs --root \"{root}\"`, shows each `hook-missing <runtime>` line and ends with STATUS=WARN on exit 1, naming the repair: copy the prompt hook entry from Skilled's own file for that runtime into the target's file. Append to the invariant that the route also runs the read-only hook-wiring check, keeping the words `check subcommand only`. Verify: `rg -c \"hook-wiring-check.cjs\" $SCY` prints at least 2 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n95: \n96: ### Wave 3\n97: - [x] T016 [P] Add `backfillRunLog({ inPath, outPath, promptFor, replay = replayOffer, brief = briefDelivered, warm })` to `OF`. It refuses with code `same_path` when `outPath` resolves to `inPath`. It calls `promptFor(rowId)` for every line before writing, so a holdout or unknown row refuses the run with no output file. For each line it takes the checkout from `locator.projectDir`, warms each checkout once with `warm(checkoutDir)` (default: `advisor_status --workspace-root <dir> --format json --no-warm-only` through that checkout's `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`), replays the offer with session id `backfill-<rowId>`, sets `briefDelivered` and writes the line with the three fields and `offerBackfilled: true`. A line with no `projectDir` gets null fields and `offerError: 'no_checkout'`. When `$SP/socket-key.txt` says `shared`, `warm` and `replay` also set `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to one short `/tmp/ssa-bf-<n>` directory per checkout. Add `OT` cases with injected `replay`, `brief` and `warm` for a two-line log, a `no_checkout` line, a refused holdout id and `same_path`. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed.\n98: - [x] T017 [P] In `S`, keep capped runs out of the start denominator: `startRows` and `started` count uncapped start rows only, and a new `cappedStartRows` counts capped start rows. Print `capped start rows <n>` on the arm line. Add one `T` case with a capped and an uncapped start row. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.opencode.pre | {startRate, cappedStartRows}'` prints a `startRate` whose denominator plus `cappedStartRows` equals the arm's start rows in `$SP/summary-before.json`.\n99: - [x] T018 [P] In `$DS/README.markdown`, add `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` and `hook-wiring-check.cjs` to the tree, the script table and the read-only table, each with its flags and exit codes. Verify: `rg -c \"codex-hook-approval-check.cjs|hook-wiring-check.cjs\" $DS/README.markdown` prints at least 4.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 4\n102: - [x] T019 In `R`, import `replayOffer` and `briefDelivered` from `OF`. In `runTurn`, after the restore and before the line is appended, call `replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId })` and add `offeredCommand` and `offeredPolicy` to the line, then add `briefDelivered` from the turn's locator (`\"unknown\"` in a dry run). Change nothing else in the turn. Verify: `node --check $R` exits 0, `rg -c \"offeredCommand|offeredPolicy|briefDelivered\" $R` prints at least 3, and `node $R --print-argv claude | jq -r 'type'` prints `array`.\n103: - [x] T020 In `S`, add `offered`, `startedOfOffered` and `offerUnknown` to each tally block. `offered` counts uncapped start rows whose line's `offeredCommand` names the row's expected command by namespace and name, `startedOfOffered` counts the started ones among them and `offerUnknown` counts uncapped start rows whose line has no `offeredCommand` key. Print `offered <X>, started <Y> of <X>, offer unknown <n>` on the arm line. Add one `T` case with an offered and a not-offered start row. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.claude.pre | {offered, startedOfOffered, offerUnknown}'` prints `{\"offered\":0,\"startedOfOffered\":0,\"offerUnknown\":12}`.\n104: \n105: ### Wave 5\n106: - [ ] T021 In `R`, add a `--backfill <run-log>` mode that needs `--out <new-log>` and calls `backfillRunLog` with a `promptFor` built from the runner's own `promptFor` and the fixture folder `resolve(dirname(<run-log>), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME)`. A refusal prints its code as JSON and exits 2. Add the mode to the usage text. Then write `$SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl` with two lines for row `cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01`, runtime `claude`, arm `pre`, exit 0, empty `changedPaths`, the first with `locator` `{\"projectDir\":\"<absolute repository root>\"}` and the second with `locator` null, and run `node $R --backfill $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl --out $SP/backfill-out.jsonl`. Verify: `jq -c '{offerBackfilled, briefDelivered, offerError, has: has(\"offeredCommand\")}' $SP/backfill-out.jsonl` prints `{\"offerBackfilled\":true,\"briefDelivered\":\"unknown\",\"offerError\":null,\"has\":true}` and then a line with `\"offerError\":\"no_checkout\"`, and `node $R --backfill $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl --out $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl` exits 2 and prints `same_path`.\n107: - [ ] T022 In `S`, after all lines are tallied, set `armIncomplete` on each runtime's arm block to true when its `runs` is lower than the largest `runs` of any runtime in the same arm, else false. End the arm's text line with `, arm incomplete` when it is true. Add one `T` case with two runtimes of two and one runs. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '[to_entries[] | select(.value.pre.armIncomplete == true) | .key]'` prints the runtimes whose `pre` run count in `$SP/summary-before.json` is below the largest.\n108: \n109: ### Wave 6\n110: - [ ] T023 In `RA`, describe the three run-line fields and that the offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn, the `--backfill` mode and its refusals, and the summary keys `offered`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerUnknown`, `cappedStartRows`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`. Verify: `rg -c \"offeredCommand|--backfill|armIncomplete|byPolicy|cappedStartRows\" $RA` prints at least 5.\n111: \n112: ### Cold-start wave C1 (only when T003 shows a fail-open)\n113: - [x] T024 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: [P] In `HR`, add a hook `advisor-warm` with concern `Start the skill advisor daemon at session start so the first prompt gets a brief`, bound for `claude` at `SessionStart` group 0 slot 8 and for `codex` at `SessionStart` group 0 slot 5, each with runner `node`, script `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, args `advisor_status --workspace-root . --format json --no-warm-only`, timeout 3 and fallback `background`. Run `node $HS`. Do not edit a generated file by hand. Verify: `node $HS --check` exits 0, `jq '[.hooks.SessionStart[].hooks[] | select(.command | test(\"skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status\"))] | length'` prints 1 for `.claude/settings.json` and for `.codex/hooks.json`, and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- .cursor/hooks.json .devin/hooks.v1.json` prints nothing.\n114: - [x] T025 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: [P] In `LCH`, set `SPECKIT_CASCADE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS` to `750` in the daemon's environment when the launcher's own environment leaves it unset, and add the name to the list of variables passed to the daemon. Verify: `node --check $LCH` exits 0 and `rg -c \"SPECKIT_CASCADE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS\" $LCH` prints at least 2.\n115: \n116: ### Cold-start wave C2\n117: - [x] T026 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: Run `node $SP/cold-start.mjs --runs 5 --warm` (`$SP/cold-start-after.txt`). Verify: its last line reads `fail-open 0 of 5`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cold-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing. If it reads more than 0, revert T024 and T025 and mark them `[B]` with the printed line.\n118: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n119: \n120: ---\n121: \n122: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n123: ## Phase 3: Verification\n124: \n125: - [ ] T027 Run every gate. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TC` exits 0, `VT` and `VO` report 0 failed, `node --test $CKT $HWT` and `DCT` report `fail 0`, `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards` reports `fail 0` and `node $HS --check` exits 0.\n126: - [ ] T028 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled .dsh | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing, and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled .dsh INSTALL-SKILLED.md .claude .codex` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n127: - [ ] T029 [C] Backfill the before arm: run `node $R --backfill $PRE --out $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl` from the repository root while the six arenas under `$TMPDIR/skilled-behaviour-arenas/pre-<runtime>` still exist at commit `2920b1abef`. Verify: `wc -l` prints the same number for both files, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- $PRE` exits 0 and `jq -r '\"\\(.runtime) \\(.briefDelivered)\"' $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl | sort | uniq -c` shows every `claude` and `pi` line as `true` and every `codex`, `devin`, `opencode` and `dsh` line as `unknown`.\n128: - [ ] T030 [C] Run `node $S --summary $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl > $SP/summary-attributed.txt`. Verify: every arm line holds `offered`, `capped start rows` and three `policy` lines, and a runtime with fewer rows than the others ends with `arm incomplete`.\n129: - [ ] T031 [C] Codex smoke: prepare one arena at this phase's commit, have the operator open `/hooks` in a Codex session there and approve each entry, then run one row through `R` with `--runtime codex --arm smoke`. Verify: `node $CK --root <arena>` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, and the rollout at the line's `locator.transcriptPath` contains `Advisor: `.\n130: - [ ] T032 Only when T031's rollout contains the brief: in `briefDelivered` (`OF`), decide `codex` from its raw rollout in the shape T031 shows, with one `OT` case built from that shape. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed. When T031's rollout holds no brief, mark this task `[x]` with `Dropped: no Codex brief in the smoke rollout`.\n131: - [ ] T033 [C] DSH smoke: in an arena at this phase's commit, run one turn through `dsh` with one route patch and the guards patch and without the lineage patch. Verify: the stored step payload contains `Advisor: `.\n132: - [ ] T034 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n133: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n138: ## Completion Criteria\n139: \n140: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`, with T024 to T026 and T032 either done or marked dropped with their measured reason\n141: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n142: - [ ] The before arm is backfilled, and the attributed summary prints an offered count for every runtime\n143: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n144: \n145: ---\n146: \n147: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n148: ## Cross-References\n149: \n150: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n151: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n152: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n153: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research.md`\n154: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n155: \n156: ---\n157: \n158: ## Verification Checklist\n159: \n160: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n161: ## Verification Protocol\n162: \n163: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n164: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n165: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n166: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n167: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n168: \n169: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n170: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n175: ## Pre-Implementation\n176: \n177: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the old-meaning summary are in `scratch/` (T001).\n178: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed the before arm is finished before wave 1 started.\n179: - [ ] CHK-003 [P1] The socket key and the cold-start measurement are recorded (T002, T003).\n180: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n181: \n182: ---\n183: \n184: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n185: ## Code Quality\n186: \n187: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0.\n188: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T028).\n189: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The two check scripts and the backfill write nothing in the home folder (T007, T028).\n190: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A failed replay yields null fields with an `offerError`, never a crash (T004).\n191: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n192: \n193: ---\n194: \n195: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n196: ## Testing Checklist\n197: \n198: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] The scorer and offer tests pass (T027).\n199: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] The floors hold (T027).\n200: - [ ] CHK-022 [P1] The doctor contract tests, the two check tests and the DSH bundle test pass (T027).\n201: - [ ] CHK-023 [P1] The backfill has as many lines as its input and leaves the input unchanged (T029).\n202: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n203: \n204: ---\n205: \n206: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n207: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n208: \n209: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `measurement`: the harness reports a rate it cannot attribute.\n210: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the start rate is listed: `rg -n \"startRate|startRows\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'`.\n211: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Every runtime with a prompt hook file is in the hook-wiring table: claude, codex, cursor and devin.\n212: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n213: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n214: \n215: ---\n216: \n217: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n218: ## Security\n219: \n220: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and the backfill refuses a holdout row id (T016).\n221: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] The Codex check prints no hash value (T007).\n222: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n227: ## Documentation\n228: \n229: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same fields and numbers.\n230: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The install guide, the DSH READMEs, the doctor scripts README and the routing-accuracy README match the code (T009, T012, T013, T018, T023).\n231: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n232: \n233: ---\n234: \n235: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n236: ## File Organization\n237: \n238: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Probes, fixtures, measurements and the attributed log live in `scratch/` only.\n239: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n240: \n241: ---\n242: \n243: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n244: ## Verification Summary\n245: \n246: | Category | Total | Verified |\n247: |----------|-------|----------|\n248: | P0 Items | 11 | 0/11 |\n249: | P1 Items | 9 | 0/9 |\n250: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n251: \n252: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n253: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/tasks.md, lines 1-253\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 10: reachability-and-attribution\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that record the offer and the brief on every run line, backfill the before arm, make the scorer attributable, add the Codex and DSH install steps with read-only doctor checks and measure the cold first prompt before changing it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reachability and attribution tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 10: reachability-and-attribution\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `S` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs`. `R` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs`. `T` is `$ADV/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts`.\n32: - `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs` (new). `OT` is `$ADV/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts` (new). `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n33: - `VT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `VO` is `npx vitest run tests/command-offer.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `PRE` is `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/scratch/runs-pre.jsonl`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n35: - `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`, the Claude prompt hook entry.\n36: - `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `DI` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs`. `DT` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/tests/bundle.test.mjs`. `DR` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md`. `DA` is `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/README.md`.\n37: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `CK` is `$DS/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` and `CKT` its test `$DS/tests/codex-hook-approval-check.test.cjs`. `HW` is `$DS/hook-wiring-check.cjs` and `HWT` its test `$DS/tests/hook-wiring-check.test.cjs`.\n38: - `RMY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-runtime-mirrors.yaml`. `SCY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-symlink-check.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs`.\n39: - `HR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/hook-registry.json`. `HS` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-hook-registrations.cjs`. `LCH` is `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-launcher.cjs`.\n40: - `FLOOR` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n41: \n42: Rules for every task:\n43: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a script and its test adds the behaviour and the one case that pins it.\n44: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A row whose split is `holdout` is never opened.\n45: - No worker starts a live model session. Live runs, arenas and operator steps are `[C]` tasks.\n46: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T028 checks it.\n47: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md` or renames `.skilled/commands/create/skill.md`.\n48: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n49: \n50: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n51: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`, `$SP/summary-before.json`), T002 (`$SP/socket-key.txt`), T003 (`$SP/cold-start.mjs`, `$SP/cold-start-before.txt`). Read-only for the repository.\n52: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that the before arm in `PRE` is finished, because T017 changes what the start rate counts.\n53: - Wave 1: T004 (`OF`, `OT`), T005 (`S`, `T`), T006 (`DI`, `DT`), T007 (`CK`, `CKT`), T008 (`HW`, `HWT`), T009 (`IG`).\n54: - Wave 2: T010 (`OF`, `OT`), T011 (`S`, `T`), T012 (`IG`), T013 (`DR`, `DA`), T014 (`RMY`), T015 (`SCY`).\n55: - Wave 3: T016 (`OF`, `OT`), T017 (`S`, `T`), T018 (`$DS/README.markdown`).\n56: - Wave 4: T019 (`R`), T020 (`S`, `T`).\n57: - Wave 5: T021 (`R`), T022 (`S`, `T`).\n58: - Wave 6: T023 (`RA`).\n59: - Cold-start wave C1, only when T003 shows a fail-open: T024 (`HR`, then `HS` regenerates `.claude/settings.json` and `.codex/hooks.json`), T025 (`LCH`).\n60: - Cold-start wave C2: T026 (`$SP/cold-start-after.txt`).\n61: - Verification, one at a time: T027, T028.\n62: - After the phase is committed: T029, T030, T031 and T033 are coordinator tasks, T032 is a worker task that waits on T031 and T034 closes the phase.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [ ] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`, `$SP/summary-before.json`). Run `FLOOR`, `TC`, `VT`, `DCT`, `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards`, `node $HS --check` and `node $S --summary $PRE --json > $SP/summary-before.json`, and write each command with its last output line into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TC` exits 0, `VT` reports 0 failed, `DCT` reports `fail 0`, the bundle test reports `fail 0`, `node $HS --check` exits 0 and `jq -r 'keys | join(\",\")' $SP/summary-before.json` prints the six runtimes.\n71: - [x] T002 Read how `$ADV/skill-advisor-cli.ts` and `LCH` name the daemon socket under `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR`, and record whether two checkouts reach two daemons (`$SP/socket-key.txt`). Verify: the file names the function and the line that derive the socket name and ends with one word, `per-checkout` or `shared`. When it says `shared`, T016 gives each checkout its own socket directory.\n72: - [x] T003 Write `$SP/cold-start.mjs` and run it once with `--runs 5` (`$SP/cold-start-before.txt`). Each run makes a fresh directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cold-'))`, with `tmpdir` from `node:os`, pipes `{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\",\"cwd\":\"<repository root>\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cold-<n>\"}` into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory, and prints the milliseconds and the first line of `additionalContext` (or `<none>`). An optional `--warm` runs `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root <root> --format json --no-warm-only` detached with the same variable and waits 3,000 ms before the hook. After each run the script sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory, then removes the directory. The last line reads `fail-open N of 5`, where a run fails open when its first line does not start with `Advisor: live` or `Advisor: stale`. Verify: the file has six lines and its last line matches `fail-open [0-5] of 5`, `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cold-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing and `ls /tmp/system-skill-advisor | wc -l` prints the same number before and after the run. When N is 0, mark T024 to T026 `[x]` with `Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5` and the median milliseconds.\n73: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n78: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n79: \n80: ### Wave 1\n81: - [x] T004 [P] Create `OF` with two exports. `parseOffer(head)` returns `{ offeredCommand, offeredPolicy }`: `offeredCommand` is the first match of `/\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/` in the head, or null, and `offeredPolicy` is `run`, `start` or `ask` when `(run)`, `(start)` or `(ask)` follows that match after optional spaces, or null. `replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = 30000 })` runs `node <checkoutDir>/$HOOK` with `cwd` `checkoutDir` and the payload shape of T003, takes the first line of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` and returns `parseOffer` of it with `offerError: null`, or null fields with `offerError` `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`. Create `OT` with cases for the heads `Advisor: live; use sk-git → /doctor:git hooks (ask) 0.82/0.12 pass.`, `Advisor: live; ambiguous: /doctor:git hooks 0.82/0.12 vs sk-git 0.86/0.16 pass.`, `Advisor: live; use command-spec-kit-plan 0.82/0.16 pass.` and a `replayOffer` call on a folder with no hook, which returns `offerError` `hook_missing`. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n82: - [x] T005 [P] In `commandParts` (`S`), when the name holds whitespace, keep the text before the first space as `name` and the rest as `route`, so `/doctor:git hooks` looks up `doctor/git.md`. Add one case to `T`: events that read `.skilled/commands/doctor/git.md` and then write give `startState(events, { expected_command: '/doctor:git hooks', expected_policy: 'run' }).started` true. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [x] T006 [P] In `apply` (`DI`), change the advisor flag to `enabled: options.promptAdvisor !== false`. In `DT`, rename the test to `prompt advisor stays on unless its row flag is false` and assert that `preStepCount({ adapters: { promptAdvisor: true } })` equals `preStepCount()` and that `preStepCount({ adapters: { promptAdvisor: false } })` is one lower. Verify: `rg -n \"promptAdvisor !== false\" $DI` prints one line, and `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards` reports `fail 0`.\n84: - [x] T007 [P] Create `CK` and `CKT`. `CK [--root <dir>] [--config <path>] [--json]` reads `<root>/.codex/hooks.json` and prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks` with exit 0 when it is absent. It finds the `UserPromptSubmit` group `g` and hook index `i` whose `command` contains `hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`, or prints `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` with exit 1. It reads `--config`, else `${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/config.toml`, and looks for a header line `[hooks.state.\"<root>/.codex/hooks.json:user_prompt_submit:<g>:<i>\"]`, with `<root>` as given or as its real path, followed by a `trusted_hash` line before the next header. Found prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` with exit 0. Otherwise it prints `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` and `repair: open /hooks in a Codex session at <root> and approve each entry`, with exit 1. A bad flag exits 2. It never prints a hash and calls no write, mkdir, rename or remove function. `CKT` covers an approved fixture, an unapproved fixture and a root with no `.codex`. Verify: `node --test $CKT` reports `pass 3` and `fail 0`, `rg -n \"writeFile|appendFile|mkdir|rename|rmSync|unlink\" $CK` prints nothing, and `shasum -a 256 ~/.codex/config.toml` prints the same digest before and after `node $CK --root .`, which prints one line starting `PASS` or `WARN`.\n85: - [x] T008 [P] Create `HW` and `HWT`. `HW --root <dir> [--json]` checks four runtimes: `claude` (`.claude/settings.json`, event `UserPromptSubmit`, marker `hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`), `codex` (`.codex/hooks.json`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`), `cursor` (`.cursor/hooks.json`, `beforeSubmitPrompt`, `hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js`) and `devin` (`.devin/hooks.v1.json`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js`). A runtime whose folder is absent is skipped. It reads the event under `hooks` or at the top level and prints `ok <runtime>` when its JSON text holds the marker, else `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, also for a missing or unreadable file. It exits 1 when any line is `hook-missing`, 0 otherwise and 2 on a bad flag or a missing root. It writes nothing. `HWT` covers a root whose `.claude/settings.json` lacks the entry, the same root with it and a root with no runtime folder. Verify: `node --test $HWT` reports `pass 3` and `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n86: - [x] T009 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the line about hyphenated commands: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved. Verify: `rg -n \"codex-hooks-approved\" $IG` prints one line and `rg -c \"trusted_hash\" $IG` prints 1.\n87: \n88: ### Wave 2\n89: - [x] T010 [P] Add `briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession })` to `OF`. For `claude` it reads `locator.transcriptPath` and returns true when a line has `type` `attachment`, `attachment.type` `hook_additional_context`, `attachment.hookEvent` `UserPromptSubmit` and content matching `/Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/`, false when the file loads without one and `\"unknown\"` when it cannot be read. For `pi` it loads the session and tests its records with the same pattern. Every other runtime returns `\"unknown\"`. Add three `OT` cases: a Claude fixture with the attachment, one without it and a `devin` locator. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed.\n90: - [x] T011 [P] In `S`, carry `wrote` (true when the run line's `changedPaths` is a non-empty array) on each scored result and add `byPolicy` to each tally block with keys `run`, `start` and `ask`, each `{ rows, capped, started, wrote }`, filled from the row's `expected_policy`. Print one text line per policy under each arm, `policy <name>: <rows> rows, <capped> capped, <started> started, <wrote> wrote`. Add one `T` case through the summary fixture. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.claude.pre.byPolicy | keys'` prints `[\"ask\",\"run\",\"start\"]`.\n91: - [x] T012 [P] In `IG`, change `any of the six runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin or Pi` to `any of the seven runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi or DeepSeek Harness (DSH)`, add DSH to the runtimes that spell commands with a hyphen and add one sentence: DSH loads the repository's guards and the skill advisor only from the patches a run names, so start it with `--patch .dsh/patches/guards.patch.yml`. Verify: `grep -ci dsh $IG` prints at least 2.\n92: - [x] T013 [P] In `DR` section 5 and in the `prompt-advisor.mjs` row of `DA`, state that the bundle registers the prompt advisor unless its row flag is `false`, that the guards patch still writes `true` and that a lineage leaf stays without a brief. Verify: `rg -n \"unless its row flag is .false.\" $DR $DA` prints two lines and `rg -n \"Off unless\" $DA` prints nothing.\n93: - [x] T014 [P] In `RMY`, add `codex_hook_approval: \".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs\"` to `upstream_assets` and one step after the `codex_hooks_installer` step: run `upstream_assets.codex_hook_approval` with `--root \"$PWD\"`. `PASS` is in sync, `WARN` sets STATUS to WARN and shows the repair line without counting as drift, and `SKIP` leaves STATUS unchanged. Keep every existing phrase. Verify: `rg -c \"codex_hook_approval\" $RMY` prints 2 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n94: - [x] T015 [P] In `SCY`, add `hook_wiring: \".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs\"` to `upstream_assets`, add a step after the engine run that runs `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/hook-wiring-check.cjs --root \"{root}\"`, shows each `hook-missing <runtime>` line and ends with STATUS=WARN on exit 1, naming the repair: copy the prompt hook entry from Skilled's own file for that runtime into the target's file. Append to the invariant that the route also runs the read-only hook-wiring check, keeping the words `check subcommand only`. Verify: `rg -c \"hook-wiring-check.cjs\" $SCY` prints at least 2 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n95: \n96: ### Wave 3\n97: - [x] T016 [P] Add `backfillRunLog({ inPath, outPath, promptFor, replay = replayOffer, brief = briefDelivered, warm })` to `OF`. It refuses with code `same_path` when `outPath` resolves to `inPath`. It calls `promptFor(rowId)` for every line before writing, so a holdout or unknown row refuses the run with no output file. For each line it takes the checkout from `locator.projectDir`, warms each checkout once with `warm(checkoutDir)` (default: `advisor_status --workspace-root <dir> --format json --no-warm-only` through that checkout's `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`), replays the offer with session id `backfill-<rowId>`, sets `briefDelivered` and writes the line with the three fields and `offerBackfilled: true`. A line with no `projectDir` gets null fields and `offerError: 'no_checkout'`. When `$SP/socket-key.txt` says `shared`, `warm` and `replay` also set `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to one short `/tmp/ssa-bf-<n>` directory per checkout. Add `OT` cases with injected `replay`, `brief` and `warm` for a two-line log, a `no_checkout` line, a refused holdout id and `same_path`. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed.\n98: - [x] T017 [P] In `S`, keep capped runs out of the start denominator: `startRows` and `started` count uncapped start rows only, and a new `cappedStartRows` counts capped start rows. Print `capped start rows <n>` on the arm line. Add one `T` case with a capped and an uncapped start row. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.opencode.pre | {startRate, cappedStartRows}'` prints a `startRate` whose denominator plus `cappedStartRows` equals the arm's start rows in `$SP/summary-before.json`.\n99: - [x] T018 [P] In `$DS/README.markdown`, add `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` and `hook-wiring-check.cjs` to the tree, the script table and the read-only table, each with its flags and exit codes. Verify: `rg -c \"codex-hook-approval-check.cjs|hook-wiring-check.cjs\" $DS/README.markdown` prints at least 4.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 4\n102: - [x] T019 In `R`, import `replayOffer` and `briefDelivered` from `OF`. In `runTurn`, after the restore and before the line is appended, call `replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId })` and add `offeredCommand` and `offeredPolicy` to the line, then add `briefDelivered` from the turn's locator (`\"unknown\"` in a dry run). Change nothing else in the turn. Verify: `node --check $R` exits 0, `rg -c \"offeredCommand|offeredPolicy|briefDelivered\" $R` prints at least 3, and `node $R --print-argv claude | jq -r 'type'` prints `array`.\n103: - [x] T020 In `S`, add `offered`, `startedOfOffered` and `offerUnknown` to each tally block. `offered` counts uncapped start rows whose line's `offeredCommand` names the row's expected command by namespace and name, `startedOfOffered` counts the started ones among them and `offerUnknown` counts uncapped start rows whose line has no `offeredCommand` key. Print `offered <X>, started <Y> of <X>, offer unknown <n>` on the arm line. Add one `T` case with an offered and a not-offered start row. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '.claude.pre | {offered, startedOfOffered, offerUnknown}'` prints `{\"offered\":0,\"startedOfOffered\":0,\"offerUnknown\":12}`.\n104: \n105: ### Wave 5\n106: - [ ] T021 In `R`, add a `--backfill <run-log>` mode that needs `--out <new-log>` and calls `backfillRunLog` with a `promptFor` built from the runner's own `promptFor` and the fixture folder `resolve(dirname(<run-log>), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME)`. A refusal prints its code as JSON and exits 2. Add the mode to the usage text. Then write `$SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl` with two lines for row `cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01`, runtime `claude`, arm `pre`, exit 0, empty `changedPaths`, the first with `locator` `{\"projectDir\":\"<absolute repository root>\"}` and the second with `locator` null, and run `node $R --backfill $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl --out $SP/backfill-out.jsonl`. Verify: `jq -c '{offerBackfilled, briefDelivered, offerError, has: has(\"offeredCommand\")}' $SP/backfill-out.jsonl` prints `{\"offerBackfilled\":true,\"briefDelivered\":\"unknown\",\"offerError\":null,\"has\":true}` and then a line with `\"offerError\":\"no_checkout\"`, and `node $R --backfill $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl --out $SP/backfill-fixture.jsonl` exits 2 and prints `same_path`.\n107: - [ ] T022 In `S`, after all lines are tallied, set `armIncomplete` on each runtime's arm block to true when its `runs` is lower than the largest `runs` of any runtime in the same arm, else false. End the arm's text line with `, arm incomplete` when it is true. Add one `T` case with two runtimes of two and one runs. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed, and `node $S --summary $PRE --json | jq -c '[to_entries[] | select(.value.pre.armIncomplete == true) | .key]'` prints the runtimes whose `pre` run count in `$SP/summary-before.json` is below the largest.\n108: \n109: ### Wave 6\n110: - [ ] T023 In `RA`, describe the three run-line fields and that the offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn, the `--backfill` mode and its refusals, and the summary keys `offered`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerUnknown`, `cappedStartRows`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`. Verify: `rg -c \"offeredCommand|--backfill|armIncomplete|byPolicy|cappedStartRows\" $RA` prints at least 5.\n111: \n112: ### Cold-start wave C1 (only when T003 shows a fail-open)\n113: - [x] T024 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: [P] In `HR`, add a hook `advisor-warm` with concern `Start the skill advisor daemon at session start so the first prompt gets a brief`, bound for `claude` at `SessionStart` group 0 slot 8 and for `codex` at `SessionStart` group 0 slot 5, each with runner `node`, script `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, args `advisor_status --workspace-root . --format json --no-warm-only`, timeout 3 and fallback `background`. Run `node $HS`. Do not edit a generated file by hand. Verify: `node $HS --check` exits 0, `jq '[.hooks.SessionStart[].hooks[] | select(.command | test(\"skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status\"))] | length'` prints 1 for `.claude/settings.json` and for `.codex/hooks.json`, and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- .cursor/hooks.json .devin/hooks.v1.json` prints nothing.\n114: - [x] T025 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: [P] In `LCH`, set `SPECKIT_CASCADE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS` to `750` in the daemon's environment when the launcher's own environment leaves it unset, and add the name to the list of variables passed to the daemon. Verify: `node --check $LCH` exits 0 and `rg -c \"SPECKIT_CASCADE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS\" $LCH` prints at least 2.\n115: \n116: ### Cold-start wave C2\n117: - [x] T026 Dropped: T003 measured fail-open 0 of 5 with a sandboxed socket (median 431 ms), and a true cold start with no daemon alive measured fail-open 0 of 5 (median 742 ms against the 2,500 ms hook budget, `scratch/cold-arena-result.txt`). Original task: Run `node $SP/cold-start.mjs --runs 5 --warm` (`$SP/cold-start-after.txt`). Verify: its last line reads `fail-open 0 of 5`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cold-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing. If it reads more than 0, revert T024 and T025 and mark them `[B]` with the printed line.\n118: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n119: \n120: ---\n121: \n122: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n123: ## Phase 3: Verification\n124: \n125: - [ ] T027 Run every gate. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TC` exits 0, `VT` and `VO` report 0 failed, `node --test $CKT $HWT` and `DCT` report `fail 0`, `node --test tests/bundle.test.mjs` in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards` reports `fail 0` and `node $HS --check` exits 0.\n126: - [ ] T028 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled .dsh | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing, and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled .dsh INSTALL-SKILLED.md .claude .codex` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n127: - [ ] T029 [C] Backfill the before arm: run `node $R --backfill $PRE --out $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl` from the repository root while the six arenas under `$TMPDIR/skilled-behaviour-arenas/pre-<runtime>` still exist at commit `2920b1abef`. Verify: `wc -l` prints the same number for both files, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- $PRE` exits 0 and `jq -r '\"\\(.runtime) \\(.briefDelivered)\"' $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl | sort | uniq -c` shows every `claude` and `pi` line as `true` and every `codex`, `devin`, `opencode` and `dsh` line as `unknown`.\n128: - [ ] T030 [C] Run `node $S --summary $SP/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl > $SP/summary-attributed.txt`. Verify: every arm line holds `offered`, `capped start rows` and three `policy` lines, and a runtime with fewer rows than the others ends with `arm incomplete`.\n129: - [ ] T031 [C] Codex smoke: prepare one arena at this phase's commit, have the operator open `/hooks` in a Codex session there and approve each entry, then run one row through `R` with `--runtime codex --arm smoke`. Verify: `node $CK --root <arena>` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, and the rollout at the line's `locator.transcriptPath` contains `Advisor: `.\n130: - [ ] T032 Only when T031's rollout contains the brief: in `briefDelivered` (`OF`), decide `codex` from its raw rollout in the shape T031 shows, with one `OT` case built from that shape. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed. When T031's rollout holds no brief, mark this task `[x]` with `Dropped: no Codex brief in the smoke rollout`.\n131: - [ ] T033 [C] DSH smoke: in an arena at this phase's commit, run one turn through `dsh` with one route patch and the guards patch and without the lineage patch. Verify: the stored step payload contains `Advisor: `.\n132: - [ ] T034 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n133: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n138: ## Completion Criteria\n139: \n140: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`, with T024 to T026 and T032 either done or marked dropped with their measured reason\n141: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n142: - [ ] The before arm is backfilled, and the attributed summary prints an offered count for every runtime\n143: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n144: \n145: ---\n146: \n147: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n148: ## Cross-References\n149: \n150: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n151: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n152: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n153: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research.md`\n154: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n155: \n156: ---\n157: \n158: ## Verification Checklist\n159: \n160: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n161: ## Verification Protocol\n162: \n163: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n164: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n165: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n166: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n167: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n168: \n169: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n170: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n175: ## Pre-Implementation\n176: \n177: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the old-meaning summary are in `scratch/` (T001).\n178: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed the before arm is finished before wave 1 started.\n179: - [ ] CHK-003 [P1] The socket key and the cold-start measurement are recorded (T002, T003).\n180: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n181: \n182: ---\n183: \n184: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n185: ## Code Quality\n186: \n187: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0.\n188: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T028).\n189: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The two check scripts and the backfill write nothing in the home folder (T007, T028).\n190: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A failed replay yields null fields with an `offerError`, never a crash (T004).\n191: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n192: \n193: ---\n194: \n195: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n196: ## Testing Checklist\n197: \n198: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] The scorer and offer tests pass (T027).\n199: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] The floors hold (T027).\n200: - [ ] CHK-022 [P1] The doctor contract tests, the two check tests and the DSH bundle test pass (T027).\n201: - [ ] CHK-023 [P1] The backfill has as many lines as its input and leaves the input unchanged (T029).\n202: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n203: \n204: ---\n205: \n206: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n207: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n208: \n209: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `measurement`: the harness reports a rate it cannot attribute.\n210: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the start rate is listed: `rg -n \"startRate|startRows\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'`.\n211: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Every runtime with a prompt hook file is in the hook-wiring table: claude, codex, cursor and devin.\n212: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n213: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n214: \n215: ---\n216: \n217: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n218: ## Security\n219: \n220: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and the backfill refuses a holdout row id (T016).\n221: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] The Codex check prints no hash value (T007).\n222: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n227: ## Documentation\n228: \n229: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same fields and numbers.\n230: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The install guide, the DSH READMEs, the doctor scripts README and the routing-accuracy README match the code (T009, T012, T013, T018, T023).\n231: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n232: \n233: ---\n234: \n235: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n236: ## File Organization\n237: \n238: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Probes, fixtures, measurements and the attributed log live in `scratch/` only.\n239: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n240: \n241: ---\n242: \n243: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n244: ## Verification Summary\n245: \n246: | Category | Total | Verified |\n247: |----------|-------|----------|\n248: | P0 Items | 11 | 0/11 |\n249: | P1 Items | 9 | 0/9 |\n250: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n251: \n252: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n253: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/tasks.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529303286,"end":1794529303458}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794529305503,"sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","part":{"id":"prt_1d248239f001OGc7A7f5UUwPzP","sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","messageID":"msg_1d24811670012plG2d3pwnESdl","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"f220f6f7e34350ce48427f17020ee48d7f461e87","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":5272,"output":102,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":12160,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794529307884,"sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","part":{"id":"prt_1d2482cec00184sKzo8OI4ocqn","sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","messageID":"msg_1d24823a6001UHFl73UNaV9Vqd","type":"step-start","snapshot":"9b13a98e9cf7f14dabe3c6a5413c1c51a5f1e890"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529312472,"sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2483b1c0024MgxRYcEfn37dk","sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","messageID":"msg_1d24823a6001UHFl73UNaV9Vqd","type":"tool","id":"call_bd8e2cddaf7c4f3ebdf93225","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1141\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: \n47: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\n48: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n49: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n50: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n51: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n52: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n53: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n54: import { briefDelivered, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n55: \n56: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n57: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n58: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n59: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n60: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n61: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n62: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n63: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n64: \n65: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n66: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n67: \n68: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n69: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n70: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n71:   chat: 300_000,\n72:   started: 900_000,\n73: });\n74: \n75: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n76: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n77: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n78: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n79: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n80: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n81: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n82: \n83: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n84: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n85: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n86: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n87:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n88: });\n89: \n90: function has(object, key) {\n91:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n92: }\n93: \n94: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n95: // Runtime argv table\n96: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: \n98: const RUNTIMES = {\n99:   claude: {\n100:     command: 'claude',\n101:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n102:     promptOnStdin: false,\n103:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n104:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n105:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n106:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n107:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n108:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n109:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n110:       '-p',\n111:       prompt,\n112:       '--model',\n113:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n114:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n115:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n116:       '--output-format',\n117:       'stream-json',\n118:       '--verbose',\n119:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n120:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n121:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n122:       '--permission-mode',\n123:       'bypassPermissions',\n124:     ],\n125:   },\n126:   codex: {\n127:     command: 'codex',\n128:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n129:     promptOnStdin: true,\n130:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n131:     // named after that thread id.\n132:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n133:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n134:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n135:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n136:     buildArgv: () => [\n137:       'exec',\n138:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n139:       '--json',\n140:       '--model',\n141:       CODEX_MODEL,\n142:       '-c',\n143:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n144:       '-c',\n145:       'service_tier=fast',\n146:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n147:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n148:       '-c',\n149:       'approval_policy=never',\n150:       '--sandbox',\n151:       'workspace-write',\n152:       '-',\n153:     ],\n154:   },\n155:   opencode: {\n156:     command: 'opencode',\n157:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n158:     promptOnStdin: false,\n159:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n160:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n161:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n162:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n163:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n164:       'run',\n165:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n166:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n167:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n168:       '--standalone',\n169:       '--model',\n170:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n171:       '--format',\n172:       'json',\n173:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n174:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n175:       '--auto',\n176:       prompt,\n177:     ],\n178:   },\n179:   pi: {\n180:     command: 'pi',\n181:     model: PI_MODEL,\n182:     promptOnStdin: false,\n183:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n184:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n185:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n186:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n187:       '-p',\n188:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n189:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n190:       '--offline',\n191:       '--model',\n192:       PI_MODEL,\n193:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n194:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n195:       '--session-id',\n196:       sessionId,\n197:       prompt,\n198:     ],\n199:   },\n200:   devin: {\n201:     command: 'devin',\n202:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n203:     promptOnStdin: false,\n204:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n205:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n206:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n207:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n208:       '-p',\n209:       '--model',\n210:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n211:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n212:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n213:       '--permission-mode',\n214:       'dangerous',\n215:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n216:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n217:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n218:       'false',\n219:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n220:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n221:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n222:       // arena instead.\n223:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n224:       '--',\n225:       prompt,\n226:     ],\n227:   },\n228:   dsh: {\n229:     command: 'dsh',\n230:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n231:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n232:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n233:     promptOnStdin: true,\n234:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n235:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n236:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n237:     buildArgv: () => [\n238:       '--profile',\n239:       'headless',\n240:       '--patch',\n241:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n242:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n243:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n244:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n245:       '--patch',\n246:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n247:       '--json',\n248:       '--permission-mode',\n249:       'workspace-write',\n250:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n251:       '--approval-policy',\n252:       'never',\n253:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n254:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n255:       // flag.\n256:       '-',\n257:     ],\n258:   },\n259: };\n260: \n261: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n262: // Session locator\n263: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n264: //\n265: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n266: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n267: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n268: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n269: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n270: // newest file in the store.\n271: \n272: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n273: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n274: // the id never changes within one turn.\n275: function streamLocator(field) {\n276:   let pending = '';\n277:   let captured = null;\n278:   return {\n279:     push(chunk) {\n280:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n281:       pending += chunk;\n282:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n283:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n284:       for (const line of lines) {\n285:         captured = readId(line, field);\n286:         if (captured !== null) return;\n287:       }\n288:     },\n289:     value() {\n290:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n291:       return captured;\n292:     },\n293:   };\n294: }\n295: \n296: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n297: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n298: function readId(line, field) {\n299:   let event;\n300:   try {\n301:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n302:   } catch {\n303:     return null;\n304:   }\n305:   const id = event?.[field];\n306:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n307: }\n308: \n309: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n310: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n311: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n312: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n313: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n314: \n315: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n316:   const source = runtime.locator;\n317:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n318:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n319:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n320:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n321:     encoding: 'utf8',\n322:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n323:   });\n324:   if (result.error) return null;\n325:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n326: }\n327: \n328: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n329: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n330: function listsStore(runtime) {\n331:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n332: }\n333: \n334: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n335: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n336: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n337: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n338:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n339:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n340:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n341: }\n342: \n343: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n344: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n345: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n346: // rather than as a failed run.\n347: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n348:   let entries;\n349:   try {\n350:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n351:   } catch {\n352:     return null;\n353:   }\n354:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n355:   return entries\n356:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n357:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n358: }\n359: \n360: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n361: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n362: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n363: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n364:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n365:   const known = new Set(before);\n366:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n367:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n368: }\n369: \n370: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n371: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n372: // the log alone.\n373: function dshHome() {\n374:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n375: }\n376: \n377: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n378: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n379: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n380: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n381: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n382: function dshSessionIds() {\n383:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n384:   try {\n385:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n386:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n387:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n388:   } catch {\n389:     return [];\n390:   }\n391: }\n392: \n393: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n394: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n395: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n396: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n397: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n398:   let entries;\n399:   try {\n400:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n401:   } catch {\n402:     return [];\n403:   }\n404:   const found = [];\n405:   for (const entry of entries) {\n406:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n407:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n408:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n409:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n410:     }\n411:   }\n412:   return found;\n413: }\n414: \n415: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n416: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n417: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n418: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n419:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n420:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n421: }\n422: \n423: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n424: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n425: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n426: // and which the reader refuses.\n427: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n428:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n429:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n430: }\n431: \n432: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n433: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n434: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n435: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n436:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n437:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n438: }\n439: \n440: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n441: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n442: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n443: // alone.\n444: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n445:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n446:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n447: }\n448: \n449: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n450:   const source = runtime.locator;\n451:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n452:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n453:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n454:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n455:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n456:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n457:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n458:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n459:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n460:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n461:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n462:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n463:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n464:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n465:     : null;\n466:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n467:   return locator;\n468: }\n469: \n470: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n471: // Worktree guards\n472: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n473: //\n474: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n475: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n476: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n477: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n478: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n479: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n480: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n481: // the damage.\n482: \n483: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n484: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n485: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n486: \n487: function git(args) {\n488:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n489:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n490:     encoding: 'utf8',\n491:   });\n492:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n493:   return result;\n494: }\n495: \n496: function gitText(args) {\n497:   const result = git(args);\n498:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n499:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n500:   }\n501:   return result.stdout;\n502: }\n503: \n504: function statusPaths() {\n505:   const paths = new Map();\n506:   for (const line of gitText(['status', '--porcelain']).split('\\n')) {\n507:     if (line.length < 4) continue;\n508:     // A rename carries both sides, and both have to be put back.\n509:     for (const path of line.slice(3).split(' -> ')) {\n510:       paths.set(path, line.slice(0, 2));\n511:     }\n512:   }\n513:   return paths;\n514: }\n515: \n516: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n517: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n518: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n519: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n520: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n521:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n522:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n523:     cwd: outDir,\n524:     encoding: 'utf8',\n525:   });\n526:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n527:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n528:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n529:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n530: }\n531: \n532: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n533:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n534:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n535:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n536: }\n537: \n538: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n539:   return [...paths.keys()]\n540:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n541:     .sort();\n542: }\n543: \n544: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n545: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n546: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n547:   const changed = [];\n548:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n549:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n550:   }\n551:   return changed.sort();\n552: }\n553: \n554: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n555: function gitSnapshot() {\n556:   return {\n557:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n558:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n559:   };\n560: }\n561: \n562: // A remote is the one piece of git state a turn could use to reach outside the\n563: // checkout, so its presence is read rather than the branch's tracking state.\n564: function remoteNames() {\n565:   return gitText(['remote'])\n566:     .split('\\n')\n567:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n568:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n569: }\n570: \n571: function pathIsTracked(path) {\n572:   return git(['ls-files', '--error-unmatch', '--', path]).status === 0;\n573: }\n574: \n575: function restorePath(path) {\n576:   if (pathIsTracked(path)) {\n577:     const result = git(['checkout', '--', path]);\n578:     return result.status === 0 ? null : `checkout ${path}: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`;\n579:   }\n580:   try {\n581:     rmSync(resolve(REPO_ROOT, path), { recursive: true, force: true });\n582:     return null;\n583:   } catch (error) {\n584:     return `delete ${path}: ${error.message}`;\n585:   }\n586: }\n587: \n588: function restorePaths(paths) {\n589:   const failures = [];\n590:   for (const path of paths) {\n591:     const failure = restorePath(path);\n592:     if (failure !== null) failures.push(failure);\n593:   }\n594:   return failures;\n595: }\n596: \n597: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n598: // Prompt source and run log\n599: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n600: \n601: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n602: class Refusal extends Error {\n603:   constructor(code, message) {\n604:     super(message);\n605:     this.code = code;\n606:   }\n607: }\n608: \n609: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n610: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n611: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n612: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n613: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n614: function findRow(rowId) {\n615:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n616:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n617:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n618:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n619:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n620:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n621:     }\n622:     return row;\n623:   }\n624:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n625: }\n626: \n627: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n628:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n629:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n630:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n631:   }\n632:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n633: }\n634: \n635: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n636: // prompt.\n637: function rowLabels() {\n638:   const labels = [];\n639:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n640:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n641:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n642:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n643:   }\n644:   return labels;\n645: }\n646: \n647: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n648: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n649: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n650:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n651:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n652: }\n653: \n654: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n655: // Prompt sets\n656: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n657: //\n658: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n659: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n660: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n661: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n662: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n663: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n664: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n665: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n666: // write under the later ones the run made.\n667: \n668: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n669: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n670: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n671: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n672:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n673:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n674:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n675: ]);\n676: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n677: \n678: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n679: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n680: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n681:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n682:   if (ids.length < count) {\n683:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n684:   }\n685:   return ids;\n686: }\n687: \n688: function chatRowIds() {\n689:   const labels = rowLabels();\n690:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n691:   const ids = [];\n692:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n693:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n694:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n695:   }\n696:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n697:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n698:   return ids;\n699: }\n700: \n701: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n702: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n703: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n704: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n705: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n706: \n707: const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n708:   {\n709:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n710:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n711:   },\n712:   {\n713:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n714:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n715:   },\n716:   {\n717:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n718:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n719:   },\n720:   {\n721:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n722:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n723:   },\n724:   {\n725:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n726:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n727:   },\n728:   {\n729:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n730:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n731:   },\n732: ]);\n733: \n734: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n735: \n736: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n737:   chat: chatRowIds,\n738:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n739: });\n740: \n741: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n742: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n743: function setNameOf(options) {\n744:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n745: }\n746: \n747: function setRowIds(setName) {\n748:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n749:   return SETS[setName]();\n750: }\n751: \n752: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n753: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n754: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n755: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n756:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n757:   return rowId;\n758: }\n759: \n760: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n761:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n762:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n763:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n764:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n765: }\n766: \n767: function fixtureDir(options) {\n768:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n769: }\n770: \n771: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n772: // machine the arm runs on.\n773: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n774:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n775:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n776:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n777: }\n778: \n779: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n780: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n781: function capForRow(rowId) {\n782:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n783: }\n784: \n785: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n786: // One turn\n787: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n788: \n789: // A dispatched worker's marker makes a CLI answer as a child session, and a child\n790: // session is not the chat start an arm measures. The rest of the environment stays,\n791: // so each CLI finds its own home, account and route.\n792: //\n793: // The network credentials are the exception, because a turn that reaches a forge\n794: // with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript can undo. The token\n795: // variables go, the GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log\n796: // rather than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper\n797: // replaces whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential\n798: // then fails at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n799: const GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS = Object.freeze(['GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN']);\n800: \n801: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n802:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n803:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n804:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n805: });\n806: \n807: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n808: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n809: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n810:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n811: }\n812: \n813: function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n814:   const env = { ...process.env };\n815:   delete env.AI_SESSION_CHILD;\n816:   for (const name of GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS) delete env[name];\n817:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n818:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n819:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n820:   return env;\n821: }\n822: \n823: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n824:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n825:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n826:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n827:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n828:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n829:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n830:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n831:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n832:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n833:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n834:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n835:       timeout: capMs,\n836:     });\n837:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n838:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n839:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n840:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n841:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n842:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n843:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n844:     }\n845:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n846:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n847:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n848:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n849:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n850:       locator.push(chunk);\n851:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n852:     });\n853:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n854:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n855:   });\n856: }\n857: \n858: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n859: // CLI\n860: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n861: \n862: class UsageError extends Error {}\n863: \n864: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n865: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n866: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n867: \n868: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n869: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n870: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n871: \n872: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n873: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n874: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n875: // change comparison by omission.\n876: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n877: \n878: const USAGE = [\n879:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n880:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n881:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n882:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n883: ].join('\\n');\n884: \n885: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n886:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n887:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n888:   }\n889:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n890:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n891:   }\n892:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n893: }\n894: \n895: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n896:   const value = argv[index];\n897:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n898:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n899:   }\n900:   return value;\n901: }\n902: \n903: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n904: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n905: function armFor(options) {\n906:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n907:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n908: }\n909: \n910: function parseOptions(argv) {\n911:   const options = {\n912:     printArgv: null,\n913:     list: null,\n914:     set: null,\n915:     runtime: null,\n916:     rowIds: [],\n917:     arm: null,\n918:     out: null,\n919:     fixture: null,\n920:     dryRun: false,\n921:   };\n922:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n923:     const token = argv[index];\n924:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n925:       index += 1;\n926:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n927:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n928:       index += 1;\n929:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n930:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n931:       index += 1;\n932:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n933:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n934:       index += 1;\n935:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n936:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n937:       index += 1;\n938:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n939:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n940:       index += 1;\n941:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n942:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n943:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n944:       }\n945:       options.arm = arm;\n946:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n947:       index += 1;\n948:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n949:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n950:       index += 1;\n951:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n952:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n953:       options.dryRun = true;\n954:     } else {\n955:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n956:     }\n957:   }\n958:   return options;\n959: }\n960: \n961: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n962: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n963: function selectMode(options) {\n964:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n965:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n966:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n967:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n968:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n969:   }\n970:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n971:   return 'run';\n972: }\n973: \n974: function writeJson(value) {\n975:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n976: }\n977: \n978: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n979:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n980: }\n981: \n982: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n983:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n984:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n985:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n986:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n987:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n988:   }\n989:   writeJson(argv);\n990: }\n991: \n992: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n993: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n994: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n995:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n996:   if (isFirst) {\n997:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start where there is no remote to push\n998:     // to. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout.\n999:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n1000:       const remotes = remoteNames();\n1001:       if (remotes.length > 0) {\n1002:         return {\n1003:           status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1004:           refusal: { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes },\n1005:         };\n1006:       }\n1007:     }\n1008:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n1009:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n1010:       return {\n1011:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1012:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n1013:       };\n1014:     }\n1015:   }\n1016: \n1017:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n1018:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1019:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n1020:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n1021:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n1022:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1023:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1024:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1025:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1026:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1027:   const reportedId = listed\n1028:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1029:     : turn.streamId;\n1030: \n1031:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1032:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1033:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1034:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1035:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1036:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1037:   const line = {\n1038:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1039:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1040:     arm: options.arm,\n1041:     locator,\n1042:     exit: turn.exit,\n1043:     changedPaths: changed,\n1044:   };\n1045: \n1046:   if (headMoved) {\n1047:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1048:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1049:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1050:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1051:   }\n1052: \n1053:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1054:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1055:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restorePaths(changed).length === 0;\n1056:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1057:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1058:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1059:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1060:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1061:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1062:     ? 'unknown'\n1063:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator });\n1064:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1065:     ...line,\n1066:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1067:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1068:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1069:     restored,\n1070:   });\n1071: \n1072:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1073:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1074:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1075:   }\n1076:   if (!restored) {\n1077:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'restore_failed', rowId: job.rowId, paths: changed } };\n1078:   }\n1079:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1080: }\n1081: \n1082: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1083:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1084:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1085:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1086:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1087:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1088:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1089:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1090:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1091:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1092:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1093:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1094:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1095:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1096:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1097:   }\n1098:   return 0;\n1099: }\n1100: \n1101: async function main(argv) {\n1102:   try {\n1103:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1104:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1105:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1106:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1107:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1108:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1109: \n1110:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1111:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1112:       return 0;\n1113:     }\n1114:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1115:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1116:       return 0;\n1117:     }\n1118:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1119:   } catch (error) {\n1120:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1121:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1122:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1123:     }\n1124:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1125:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1126:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1127:     }\n1128:     throw error;\n1129:   }\n1130: }\n1131: \n1132: try {\n1133:   process.exitCode = await main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1134: } catch (error) {\n1135:   if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1136:     process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n`);\n1137:     process.exitCode = EXIT_REFUSED;\n1138:   } else {\n1139:     throw error;\n1140:   }\n1141: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1141\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: \n47: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\n48: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n49: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n50: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n51: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n52: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n53: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n54: import { briefDelivered, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n55: \n56: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n57: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n58: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n59: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n60: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n61: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n62: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n63: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n64: \n65: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n66: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n67: \n68: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n69: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n70: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n71:   chat: 300_000,\n72:   started: 900_000,\n73: });\n74: \n75: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n76: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n77: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n78: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n79: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n80: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n81: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n82: \n83: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n84: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n85: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n86: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n87:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n88: });\n89: \n90: function has(object, key) {\n91:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n92: }\n93: \n94: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n95: // Runtime argv table\n96: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: \n98: const RUNTIMES = {\n99:   claude: {\n100:     command: 'claude',\n101:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n102:     promptOnStdin: false,\n103:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n104:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n105:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n106:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n107:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n108:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n109:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n110:       '-p',\n111:       prompt,\n112:       '--model',\n113:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n114:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n115:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n116:       '--output-format',\n117:       'stream-json',\n118:       '--verbose',\n119:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n120:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n121:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n122:       '--permission-mode',\n123:       'bypassPermissions',\n124:     ],\n125:   },\n126:   codex: {\n127:     command: 'codex',\n128:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n129:     promptOnStdin: true,\n130:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n131:     // named after that thread id.\n132:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n133:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n134:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n135:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n136:     buildArgv: () => [\n137:       'exec',\n138:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n139:       '--json',\n140:       '--model',\n141:       CODEX_MODEL,\n142:       '-c',\n143:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n144:       '-c',\n145:       'service_tier=fast',\n146:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n147:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n148:       '-c',\n149:       'approval_policy=never',\n150:       '--sandbox',\n151:       'workspace-write',\n152:       '-',\n153:     ],\n154:   },\n155:   opencode: {\n156:     command: 'opencode',\n157:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n158:     promptOnStdin: false,\n159:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n160:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n161:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n162:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n163:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n164:       'run',\n165:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n166:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n167:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n168:       '--standalone',\n169:       '--model',\n170:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n171:       '--format',\n172:       'json',\n173:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n174:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n175:       '--auto',\n176:       prompt,\n177:     ],\n178:   },\n179:   pi: {\n180:     command: 'pi',\n181:     model: PI_MODEL,\n182:     promptOnStdin: false,\n183:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n184:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n185:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n186:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n187:       '-p',\n188:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n189:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n190:       '--offline',\n191:       '--model',\n192:       PI_MODEL,\n193:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n194:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n195:       '--session-id',\n196:       sessionId,\n197:       prompt,\n198:     ],\n199:   },\n200:   devin: {\n201:     command: 'devin',\n202:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n203:     promptOnStdin: false,\n204:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n205:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n206:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n207:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n208:       '-p',\n209:       '--model',\n210:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n211:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n212:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n213:       '--permission-mode',\n214:       'dangerous',\n215:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n216:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n217:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n218:       'false',\n219:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n220:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n221:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n222:       // arena instead.\n223:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n224:       '--',\n225:       prompt,\n226:     ],\n227:   },\n228:   dsh: {\n229:     command: 'dsh',\n230:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n231:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n232:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n233:     promptOnStdin: true,\n234:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n235:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n236:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n237:     buildArgv: () => [\n238:       '--profile',\n239:       'headless',\n240:       '--patch',\n241:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n242:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n243:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n244:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n245:       '--patch',\n246:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n247:       '--json',\n248:       '--permission-mode',\n249:       'workspace-write',\n250:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n251:       '--approval-policy',\n252:       'never',\n253:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n254:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n255:       // flag.\n256:       '-',\n257:     ],\n258:   },\n259: };\n260: \n261: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n262: // Session locator\n263: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n264: //\n265: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n266: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n267: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n268: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n269: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n270: // newest file in the store.\n271: \n272: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n273: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n274: // the id never changes within one turn.\n275: function streamLocator(field) {\n276:   let pending = '';\n277:   let captured = null;\n278:   return {\n279:     push(chunk) {\n280:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n281:       pending += chunk;\n282:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n283:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n284:       for (const line of lines) {\n285:         captured = readId(line, field);\n286:         if (captured !== null) return;\n287:       }\n288:     },\n289:     value() {\n290:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n291:       return captured;\n292:     },\n293:   };\n294: }\n295: \n296: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n297: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n298: function readId(line, field) {\n299:   let event;\n300:   try {\n301:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n302:   } catch {\n303:     return null;\n304:   }\n305:   const id = event?.[field];\n306:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n307: }\n308: \n309: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n310: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n311: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n312: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n313: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n314: \n315: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n316:   const source = runtime.locator;\n317:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n318:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n319:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n320:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n321:     encoding: 'utf8',\n322:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n323:   });\n324:   if (result.error) return null;\n325:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n326: }\n327: \n328: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n329: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n330: function listsStore(runtime) {\n331:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n332: }\n333: \n334: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n335: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n336: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n337: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n338:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n339:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n340:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n341: }\n342: \n343: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n344: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n345: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n346: // rather than as a failed run.\n347: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n348:   let entries;\n349:   try {\n350:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n351:   } catch {\n352:     return null;\n353:   }\n354:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n355:   return entries\n356:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n357:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n358: }\n359: \n360: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n361: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n362: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n363: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n364:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n365:   const known = new Set(before);\n366:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n367:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n368: }\n369: \n370: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n371: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n372: // the log alone.\n373: function dshHome() {\n374:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n375: }\n376: \n377: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n378: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n379: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n380: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n381: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n382: function dshSessionIds() {\n383:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n384:   try {\n385:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n386:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n387:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n388:   } catch {\n389:     return [];\n390:   }\n391: }\n392: \n393: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n394: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n395: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n396: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n397: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n398:   let entries;\n399:   try {\n400:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n401:   } catch {\n402:     return [];\n403:   }\n404:   const found = [];\n405:   for (const entry of entries) {\n406:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n407:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n408:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n409:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n410:     }\n411:   }\n412:   return found;\n413: }\n414: \n415: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n416: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n417: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n418: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n419:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n420:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n421: }\n422: \n423: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n424: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n425: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n426: // and which the reader refuses.\n427: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n428:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n429:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n430: }\n431: \n432: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n433: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n434: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n435: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n436:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n437:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n438: }\n439: \n440: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n441: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n442: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n443: // alone.\n444: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n445:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n446:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n447: }\n448: \n449: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n450:   const source = runtime.locator;\n451:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n452:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n453:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n454:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n455:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n456:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n457:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n458:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n459:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n460:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n461:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n462:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n463:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n464:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n465:     : null;\n466:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n467:   return locator;\n468: }\n469: \n470: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n471: // Worktree guards\n472: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n473: //\n474: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n475: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n476: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n477: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n478: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n479: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n480: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n481: // the damage.\n482: \n483: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n484: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n485: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n486: \n487: function git(args) {\n488:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n489:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n490:     encoding: 'utf8',\n491:   });\n492:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n493:   return result;\n494: }\n495: \n496: function gitText(args) {\n497:   const result = git(args);\n498:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n499:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n500:   }\n501:   return result.stdout;\n502: }\n503: \n504: function statusPaths() {\n505:   const paths = new Map();\n506:   for (const line of gitText(['status', '--porcelain']).split('\\n')) {\n507:     if (line.length < 4) continue;\n508:     // A rename carries both sides, and both have to be put back.\n509:     for (const path of line.slice(3).split(' -> ')) {\n510:       paths.set(path, line.slice(0, 2));\n511:     }\n512:   }\n513:   return paths;\n514: }\n515: \n516: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n517: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n518: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n519: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n520: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n521:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n522:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n523:     cwd: outDir,\n524:     encoding: 'utf8',\n525:   });\n526:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n527:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n528:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n529:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n530: }\n531: \n532: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n533:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n534:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n535:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n536: }\n537: \n538: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n539:   return [...paths.keys()]\n540:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n541:     .sort();\n542: }\n543: \n544: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n545: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n546: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n547:   const changed = [];\n548:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n549:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n550:   }\n551:   return changed.sort();\n552: }\n553: \n554: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n555: function gitSnapshot() {\n556:   return {\n557:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n558:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n559:   };\n560: }\n561: \n562: // A remote is the one piece of git state a turn could use to reach outside the\n563: // checkout, so its presence is read rather than the branch's tracking state.\n564: function remoteNames() {\n565:   return gitText(['remote'])\n566:     .split('\\n')\n567:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n568:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n569: }\n570: \n571: function pathIsTracked(path) {\n572:   return git(['ls-files', '--error-unmatch', '--', path]).status === 0;\n573: }\n574: \n575: function restorePath(path) {\n576:   if (pathIsTracked(path)) {\n577:     const result = git(['checkout', '--', path]);\n578:     return result.status === 0 ? null : `checkout ${path}: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`;\n579:   }\n580:   try {\n581:     rmSync(resolve(REPO_ROOT, path), { recursive: true, force: true });\n582:     return null;\n583:   } catch (error) {\n584:     return `delete ${path}: ${error.message}`;\n585:   }\n586: }\n587: \n588: function restorePaths(paths) {\n589:   const failures = [];\n590:   for (const path of paths) {\n591:     const failure = restorePath(path);\n592:     if (failure !== null) failures.push(failure);\n593:   }\n594:   return failures;\n595: }\n596: \n597: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n598: // Prompt source and run log\n599: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n600: \n601: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n602: class Refusal extends Error {\n603:   constructor(code, message) {\n604:     super(message);\n605:     this.code = code;\n606:   }\n607: }\n608: \n609: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n610: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n611: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n612: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n613: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n614: function findRow(rowId) {\n615:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n616:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n617:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n618:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n619:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n620:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n621:     }\n622:     return row;\n623:   }\n624:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n625: }\n626: \n627: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n628:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n629:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n630:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n631:   }\n632:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n633: }\n634: \n635: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n636: // prompt.\n637: function rowLabels() {\n638:   const labels = [];\n639:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n640:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n641:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n642:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n643:   }\n644:   return labels;\n645: }\n646: \n647: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n648: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n649: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n650:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n651:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n652: }\n653: \n654: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n655: // Prompt sets\n656: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n657: //\n658: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n659: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n660: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n661: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n662: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n663: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n664: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n665: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n666: // write under the later ones the run made.\n667: \n668: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n669: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n670: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n671: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n672:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n673:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n674:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n675: ]);\n676: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n677: \n678: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n679: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n680: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n681:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n682:   if (ids.length < count) {\n683:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n684:   }\n685:   return ids;\n686: }\n687: \n688: function chatRowIds() {\n689:   const labels = rowLabels();\n690:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n691:   const ids = [];\n692:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n693:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n694:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n695:   }\n696:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n697:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n698:   return ids;\n699: }\n700: \n701: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n702: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n703: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n704: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n705: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n706: \n707: const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n708:   {\n709:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n710:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n711:   },\n712:   {\n713:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n714:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n715:   },\n716:   {\n717:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n718:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n719:   },\n720:   {\n721:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n722:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n723:   },\n724:   {\n725:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n726:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n727:   },\n728:   {\n729:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n730:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n731:   },\n732: ]);\n733: \n734: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n735: \n736: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n737:   chat: chatRowIds,\n738:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n739: });\n740: \n741: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n742: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n743: function setNameOf(options) {\n744:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n745: }\n746: \n747: function setRowIds(setName) {\n748:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n749:   return SETS[setName]();\n750: }\n751: \n752: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n753: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n754: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n755: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n756:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n757:   return rowId;\n758: }\n759: \n760: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n761:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n762:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n763:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n764:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n765: }\n766: \n767: function fixtureDir(options) {\n768:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n769: }\n770: \n771: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n772: // machine the arm runs on.\n773: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n774:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n775:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n776:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n777: }\n778: \n779: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n780: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n781: function capForRow(rowId) {\n782:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n783: }\n784: \n785: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n786: // One turn\n787: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n788: \n789: // A dispatched worker's marker makes a CLI answer as a child session, and a child\n790: // session is not the chat start an arm measures. The rest of the environment stays,\n791: // so each CLI finds its own home, account and route.\n792: //\n793: // The network credentials are the exception, because a turn that reaches a forge\n794: // with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript can undo. The token\n795: // variables go, the GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log\n796: // rather than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper\n797: // replaces whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential\n798: // then fails at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n799: const GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS = Object.freeze(['GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN']);\n800: \n801: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n802:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n803:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n804:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n805: });\n806: \n807: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n808: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n809: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n810:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n811: }\n812: \n813: function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n814:   const env = { ...process.env };\n815:   delete env.AI_SESSION_CHILD;\n816:   for (const name of GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS) delete env[name];\n817:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n818:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n819:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n820:   return env;\n821: }\n822: \n823: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n824:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n825:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n826:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n827:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n828:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n829:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n830:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n831:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n832:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n833:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n834:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n835:       timeout: capMs,\n836:     });\n837:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n838:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n839:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n840:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n841:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n842:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n843:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n844:     }\n845:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n846:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n847:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n848:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n849:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n850:       locator.push(chunk);\n851:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n852:     });\n853:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n854:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n855:   });\n856: }\n857: \n858: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n859: // CLI\n860: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n861: \n862: class UsageError extends Error {}\n863: \n864: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n865: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n866: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n867: \n868: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n869: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n870: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n871: \n872: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n873: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n874: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n875: // change comparison by omission.\n876: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n877: \n878: const USAGE = [\n879:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n880:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n881:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n882:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n883: ].join('\\n');\n884: \n885: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n886:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n887:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n888:   }\n889:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n890:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n891:   }\n892:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n893: }\n894: \n895: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n896:   const value = argv[index];\n897:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n898:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n899:   }\n900:   return value;\n901: }\n902: \n903: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n904: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n905: function armFor(options) {\n906:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n907:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n908: }\n909: \n910: function parseOptions(argv) {\n911:   const options = {\n912:     printArgv: null,\n913:     list: null,\n914:     set: null,\n915:     runtime: null,\n916:     rowIds: [],\n917:     arm: null,\n918:     out: null,\n919:     fixture: null,\n920:     dryRun: false,\n921:   };\n922:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n923:     const token = argv[index];\n924:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n925:       index += 1;\n926:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n927:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n928:       index += 1;\n929:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n930:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n931:       index += 1;\n932:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n933:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n934:       index += 1;\n935:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n936:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n937:       index += 1;\n938:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n939:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n940:       index += 1;\n941:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n942:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n943:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n944:       }\n945:       options.arm = arm;\n946:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n947:       index += 1;\n948:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n949:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n950:       index += 1;\n951:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n952:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n953:       options.dryRun = true;\n954:     } else {\n955:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n956:     }\n957:   }\n958:   return options;\n959: }\n960: \n961: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n962: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n963: function selectMode(options) {\n964:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n965:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n966:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n967:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n968:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n969:   }\n970:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n971:   return 'run';\n972: }\n973: \n974: function writeJson(value) {\n975:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n976: }\n977: \n978: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n979:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n980: }\n981: \n982: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n983:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n984:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n985:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n986:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n987:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n988:   }\n989:   writeJson(argv);\n990: }\n991: \n992: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n993: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n994: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n995:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n996:   if (isFirst) {\n997:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start where there is no remote to push\n998:     // to. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout.\n999:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n1000:       const remotes = remoteNames();\n1001:       if (remotes.length > 0) {\n1002:         return {\n1003:           status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1004:           refusal: { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes },\n1005:         };\n1006:       }\n1007:     }\n1008:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n1009:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n1010:       return {\n1011:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1012:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n1013:       };\n1014:     }\n1015:   }\n1016: \n1017:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n1018:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1019:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n1020:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n1021:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n1022:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1023:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1024:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1025:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1026:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1027:   const reportedId = listed\n1028:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1029:     : turn.streamId;\n1030: \n1031:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1032:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1033:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1034:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1035:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1036:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1037:   const line = {\n1038:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1039:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1040:     arm: options.arm,\n1041:     locator,\n1042:     exit: turn.exit,\n1043:     changedPaths: changed,\n1044:   };\n1045: \n1046:   if (headMoved) {\n1047:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1048:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1049:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1050:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1051:   }\n1052: \n1053:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1054:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1055:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restorePaths(changed).length === 0;\n1056:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1057:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1058:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1059:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1060:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1061:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1062:     ? 'unknown'\n1063:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator });\n1064:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1065:     ...line,\n1066:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1067:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1068:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1069:     restored,\n1070:   });\n1071: \n1072:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1073:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1074:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1075:   }\n1076:   if (!restored) {\n1077:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'restore_failed', rowId: job.rowId, paths: changed } };\n1078:   }\n1079:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1080: }\n1081: \n1082: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1083:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1084:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1085:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1086:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1087:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1088:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1089:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1090:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1091:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1092:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1093:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1094:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1095:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1096:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1097:   }\n1098:   return 0;\n1099: }\n1100: \n1101: async function main(argv) {\n1102:   try {\n1103:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1104:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1105:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1106:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1107:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1108:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1109: \n1110:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1111:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1112:       return 0;\n1113:     }\n1114:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1115:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1116:       return 0;\n1117:     }\n1118:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1119:   } catch (error) {\n1120:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1121:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1122:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1123:     }\n1124:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1125:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1126:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1127:     }\n1128:     throw error;\n1129:   }\n1130: }\n1131: \n1132: try {\n1133:   process.exitCode = await main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1134: } catch (error) {\n1135:   if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1136:     process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n`);\n1137:     process.exitCode = EXIT_REFUSED;\n1138:   } else {\n1139:     throw error;\n1140:   }\n1141: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529311516,"end":1794529312472}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529312510,"sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2483d38001ewcFlS14C4zlUM","sessionID":"ses_e2db801a6ffedq2jGg0eorCLsO","messageID":"msg_1d24823a6001UHFl73UNaV9Vqd","type":"tool","id":"call_115a9ed99a5a4dc18adc7b3f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-328\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: \n29: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n30: \n31: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n32: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n33: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n34: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n35: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n36: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n37: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n38: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n39: \n40: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n41:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n42: }\n43: \n44: /**\n45:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n46:  *\n47:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n48:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n49:  *\n50:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n51:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n52:  */\n53: export function parseOffer(head) {\n54:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n55:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n56:   if (!match) {\n57:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n58:   }\n59:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n60:   return {\n61:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n62:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n63:   };\n64: }\n65: \n66: /**\n67:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n68:  *\n69:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n70:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n71:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n72:  *\n73:  * @param {object} options\n74:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n75:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n76:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n77:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n78:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n79:  */\n80: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n81:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n82:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n83:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n84:   }\n85: \n86:   const payload = {\n87:     prompt,\n88:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n89:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n90:     session_id: sessionId,\n91:   };\n92: \n93:   let result;\n94:   try {\n95:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n96:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n97:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n98:       encoding: 'utf8',\n99:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n100:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n101:     });\n102:   } catch {\n103:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n104:   }\n105: \n106:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n107:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n108:   }\n109: \n110:   let output;\n111:   try {\n112:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n113:   } catch {\n114:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n115:   }\n116: \n117:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n118:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n119:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n120:   }\n121: \n122:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n123:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n124:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n125:   }\n126: \n127:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n128: }\n129: \n130: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n131:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n132:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n133:   return content.some((part) => {\n134:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n135:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n136:   });\n137: }\n138: \n139: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n140: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n141: // whichever block carries it.\n142: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n143:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n144:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n145: }\n146: \n147: /**\n148:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n149:  *\n150:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n151:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi keeps delivery in its session records\n152:  * and needs the caller's reader. Every other runtime has no confirmed record of\n153:  * delivery, so its answer stays `unknown` and never counts as a miss.\n154:  *\n155:  * @param {object} options\n156:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n157:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n158:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n159:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n160:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n161:  */\n162: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession } = {}) {\n163:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n164:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n165:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n166:       return 'unknown';\n167:     }\n168: \n169:     let raw;\n170:     try {\n171:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n172:     } catch {\n173:       return 'unknown';\n174:     }\n175: \n176:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n177:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n178:       let entry;\n179:       try {\n180:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n181:       } catch {\n182:         continue;\n183:       }\n184:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n185:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n186:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n187:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n188:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n189:     }\n190:     return false;\n191:   }\n192: \n193:   if (runtime === 'pi') {\n194:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n195:     let loaded;\n196:     try {\n197:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n198:     } catch {\n199:       return 'unknown';\n200:     }\n201:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n202:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records);\n203:   }\n204: \n205:   return 'unknown';\n206: }\n207: \n208: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n209: // Run-log backfill\n210: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n211: \n212: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n213: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n214: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n215: // own error instead of losing the line.\n216: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n217:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n218:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n219:   spawnSync(\n220:     'node',\n221:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n222:     {\n223:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n224:       encoding: 'utf8',\n225:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n226:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n227:     },\n228:   );\n229: }\n230: \n231: function refused(code, detail) {\n232:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n233: }\n234: \n235: /**\n236:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n237:  *\n238:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n239:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n240:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n241:  * log is still written.\n242:  *\n243:  * @param {object} options\n244:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n245:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n246:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n247:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n248:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n249:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n250:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n251:  *   injectable for tests.\n252:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n253:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n254:  */\n255: export async function backfillRunLog({\n256:   inPath,\n257:   outPath,\n258:   promptFor,\n259:   replay = replayOffer,\n260:   brief = briefDelivered,\n261:   warm = warmCheckout,\n262: }) {\n263:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n264:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n265:   }\n266: \n267:   const rows = [];\n268:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n269:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n270:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n271:   }\n272: \n273:   const prompts = [];\n274:   for (const row of rows) {\n275:     try {\n276:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n277:     } catch (error) {\n278:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n279:     }\n280:   }\n281: \n282:   const warmed = new Set();\n283:   const output = [];\n284:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n285:     const line = rows[index];\n286:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n287:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n288:       ? line.locator\n289:       : null;\n290:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n291:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n292: \n293:     let offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n294:     if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n295:       if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n296:         warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n297:         try {\n298:           await warm(checkoutDir);\n299:         } catch {\n300:           // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n301:         }\n302:       }\n303:       offer = await replay({\n304:         checkoutDir,\n305:         prompt: prompts[index],\n306:         sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n307:       });\n308:     }\n309: \n310:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n311:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n312:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n313:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n314: \n315:     output.push({\n316:       ...line,\n317:       offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n318:       offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n319:       offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n320:       briefDelivered: delivered,\n321:       offerBackfilled: true,\n322:     });\n323:   }\n324: \n325:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n326:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n327:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n328: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-328\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: \n29: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n30: \n31: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n32: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n33: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n34: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n35: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n36: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n37: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n38: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n39: \n40: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n41:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n42: }\n43: \n44: /**\n45:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n46:  *\n47:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n48:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n49:  *\n50:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n51:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n52:  */\n53: export function parseOffer(head) {\n54:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n55:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n56:   if (!match) {\n57:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n58:   }\n59:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n60:   return {\n61:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n62:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n63:   };\n64: }\n65: \n66: /**\n67:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n68:  *\n69:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n70:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n71:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n72:  *\n73:  * @param {object} options\n74:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n75:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n76:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n77:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n78:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n79:  */\n80: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n81:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n82:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n83:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n84:   }\n85: \n86:   const payload = {\n87:     prompt,\n88:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n89:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n90:     session_id: sessionId,\n91:   };\n92: \n93:   let result;\n94:   try {\n95:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n96:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n97:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n98:       encoding: 'utf8',\n99:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n100:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n101:     });\n102:   } catch {\n103:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n104:   }\n105: \n106:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n107:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n108:   }\n109: \n110:   let output;\n111:   try {\n112:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n113:   } catch {\n114:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n115:   }\n116: \n117:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n118:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n119:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n120:   }\n121: \n122:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n123:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n124:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n125:   }\n126: \n127:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n128: }\n129: \n130: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n131:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n132:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n133:   return content.some((part) => {\n134:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n135:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n136:   });\n137: }\n138: \n139: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n140: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n141: // whichever block carries it.\n142: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n143:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n144:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n145: }\n146: \n147: /**\n148:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n149:  *\n150:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n151:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi keeps delivery in its session records\n152:  * and needs the caller's reader. Every other runtime has no confirmed record of\n153:  * delivery, so its answer stays `unknown` and never counts as a miss.\n154:  *\n155:  * @param {object} options\n156:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n157:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n158:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n159:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n160:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n161:  */\n162: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession } = {}) {\n163:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n164:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n165:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n166:       return 'unknown';\n167:     }\n168: \n169:     let raw;\n170:     try {\n171:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n172:     } catch {\n173:       return 'unknown';\n174:     }\n175: \n176:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n177:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n178:       let entry;\n179:       try {\n180:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n181:       } catch {\n182:         continue;\n183:       }\n184:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n185:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n186:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n187:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n188:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n189:     }\n190:     return false;\n191:   }\n192: \n193:   if (runtime === 'pi') {\n194:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n195:     let loaded;\n196:     try {\n197:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n198:     } catch {\n199:       return 'unknown';\n200:     }\n201:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n202:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records);\n203:   }\n204: \n205:   return 'unknown';\n206: }\n207: \n208: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n209: // Run-log backfill\n210: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n211: \n212: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n213: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n214: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n215: // own error instead of losing the line.\n216: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n217:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n218:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n219:   spawnSync(\n220:     'node',\n221:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n222:     {\n223:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n224:       encoding: 'utf8',\n225:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n226:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n227:     },\n228:   );\n229: }\n230: \n231: function refused(code, detail) {\n232:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n233: }\n234: \n235: /**\n236:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n237:  *\n238:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n239:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n240:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n241:  * log is still written.\n242:  *\n243:  * @param {object} options\n244:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n245:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n246:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n247:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n248:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n249:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n250:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n251:  *   injectable for tests.\n252:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n253:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n254:  */\n255: export async function backfillRunLog({\n256:   inPath,\n257:   outPath,\n258:   promptFor,\n259:   replay = replayOffer,\n260:   brief = briefDelivered,\n261:   warm = warmCheckout,\n262: }) {\n263:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n264:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n265:   }\n266: \n267:   const rows = [];\n268:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n269:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n270:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n271:   }\n272: \n273:   const prompts = [];\n274:   for (const row of rows) {\n275:     try {\n276:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n277:     } catch (error) {\n278:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n279:     }\n280:   }\n281: \n282:   const warmed = new Set();\n283:   const output = [];\n284:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n285:     const line = rows[index];\n286:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n287:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n288:       ? line.locator\n289:       : null;\n290:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n291:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n292: \n293:     let offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n294:     if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n295:       if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n296:         warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n297:         try {\n298:           await warm(checkoutDir);\n299:         } catch {\n300:           // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n301:         }\n302:       }\n303:       offer = await replay({\n304:         checkoutDir,\n305:         prompt: prompts[index],\n306:         sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n307:       });\n308:     }\n309: \n310:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n311:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n312:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n313:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n314: \n315:     output.push({\n316:       ...line,\n317:       offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n318:       offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n319:       offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n320:       briefDelivered: delivered,\n321:       offerBackfilled: true,\n322:     });\n323:   }\n324: \n325:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n326:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n327:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n328: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529312056,"end":1794529312510}}}}
